The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (ret) {
    xe_sriov_packet_free(*data);
    return ret;
}
```
Reason:
When `xe_sriov_pf_migration_restore_produce()` returns an error, the memory pointed to by `*data` is freed but `*data` is not set to NULL. Since `*data` remains a dangling pointer, subsequent calls to `xe_sriov_packet_write_single()` will see it as non-NULL (via the `IS_ERR_OR_NULL(*data)` check) and attempt to use the freed memory, leading to a use-after-free vulnerability.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (ret) {
    xe_sriov_packet_free(*data);
    *data = NULL;
    return ret;
}
```
Reason:
Setting `*data = NULL` after freeing the packet ensures that any subsequent calls to `xe_sriov_packet_write_single()` will detect that no valid packet exists and will allocate a new one via `xe_sriov_packet_alloc()`, preventing the dereference of freed memory.

